{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgh2baVCqbK9gysobS4ep","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Wr5Tnw7bCCJpwNG9wJJQDp","name":"Read","input":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174752__xfb4127/work/PLAN.md","offset":1,"limit":140},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1181,"cache_read_input_tokens":467983,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1181},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4e34a208-b0be-46a3-80b7-32b0075578cc","uuid":"993c4a96-27a8-44f5-b5c9-a19d46e1e96e","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:34:38.936Z","request_id":"req_011Cgh2baDpSG2jjme8YuQa8","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01Wr5Tnw7bCCJpwNG9wJJQDp":{"file_path":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174752__xfb4127/work/PLAN.md","offset":1,"limit":140}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Wr5Tnw7bCCJpwNG9wJJQDp","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# SKILLER7\n2\t\n3\t*Seven Killers, One Mountain.*\n4\t\n5\t**Title.** Picked the way TASK.md says: 50 playful riffs on \"Killer7\" were brainstormed silently, then one was drawn\n6\twith a real random number generator (Python `secrets.randbelow(50)`, OS entropy): index 13, **Skiller7**. It reads three\n7\tways at once (SKI + KILLER + SKILL), and the whole design leans on that. In file and folder names, GameTitle is\n8\t`Skiller7` and SOURCE is `Killer7`.\n9\t\n10\t**Pitch.** Piz Nada is a neutral micro-state on one mountain, and it has snowed there for a hundred years. On the top\n11\tfloor of its sanatorium, in Room 7, a 104-year-old former ski champion called Oma Holle lies under a mountain of\n12\tfeatherbed. When she shakes it, seven killers step out of her wardrobe. The Canton's Department of Discretion sends\n13\tthem \"guests\" to check out: agents of Mister Föhn, the warm wind that makes people laugh until they disappear. You move\n14\ton rails through a grand hotel, a ski village, a summit conference, a black ski run, a gondola and a glacier vault.\n15\tThen you stop, look through your persona's eyes, scan for the laughing, invisible Whiteouts, and shoot their Warm Spot\n16\tbefore they reach you.\n17\t\n18\t---\n19\t\n20\t## 0. How to use this plan\n21\t\n22\t- **TASK.md + PLAN.md are the whole brief.** TASK.md decides deliverables and process. This plan decides design. Where\n23\t  this plan gives a number, use it as the default and expose the important ones as live dev sliders (§7.22).\n24\t- **Everything in the game is original.** We take Killer7's *structure* (rail movement, stop-and-aim first-person\n25\t  scanning, weak-point instant kills, one body with several killer personas, a save room, a blood economy, cryptic\n26\t  hint-givers, chapters called targets, bosses with odd rules) and its *tone*. We do not use its names, characters,\n27\t  art, music, levels or look.\n28\t- **Build in this order:** a playable greybox golden path first, then systems to full depth, then content, then polish\n29\t  (§12). The scope is sized to be built whole. TASK.md forbids TODOs, placeholders and stubs, so build every system and\n30\t  piece of content in this plan.\n31\t- **Mark every engine change** in the engine files with `// S7 PATCH:` and a reason, and keep a list for the\n32\t  hand-back's Engine Feedback (§16).\n33\t\n34\t---\n35\t\n36\t## 1. At a glance\n37\t\n38\t| | |\n39\t|---|---|\n40\t| Title | **SKILLER7** (shown with that spelling; `Skiller7` in file names) |\n41\t| Tagline | Seven Killers, One Mountain |\n42\t| Source | Killer7 (Grasshopper Manufacture / Capcom, 2005) |\n43\t| Genre | On-rails action-adventure shooter: rail movement, first-person scan-and-shoot, persona-switch puzzles, boss fights. AI Hub catalogue genre: **Shooter** |\n44\t| Perspective | Third-person cinematic rail cameras (fixed, dolly, follow and crane shots that hard-cut) while moving; **first person** while aiming; a schematic top-down map in the pause menu |\n45\t| Players | 1 in the campaign. 1–4 in **Echo Run** (AI Hub ghost mode; solo, you race your own recorded echo) |\n46\t| Length | about 45–60 min: Target 00 \"Turn-Down Service\" (about 12 min), Target 01 \"Après\" (35–45 min), epilogue. Echo Run rounds take about 2 min |\n47\t| Engine | my-3D2dge **v0.85.2** handoff (the 2D engine runs the game) plus the engine's own **3D module v0.85.2** (three.js r182, Rapier 0.19.3 from a CDN) |\n48\t| Look | The engine's native style: PS1-resolution picture (`'pixels'`), characters as pixel-art rigs drawn on cards, the 3D module's **Comic cel** filter (flat bands of light, ink lines). Stark white snow, black shadows, saturated red |\n49\t| Animation | The engine's procedural rigs (`E.Humanoid`, `E.Blob`, `E.MOVES`, poses), extended with new hats, accessories, weapons, skis and poses; first-person weapon models with the overlay pen |\n50\t| Audio | All in code: engine chip-synth songs, engine SFX voices, our own WebAudio laughter synth and Animalese-style gibberish voices |\n51\t| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1, `ghost` mode (Echo Run). On its own, the game runs without the hub |\n52\t| Ship | `Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752.html` and `exit-interview.json` in this folder. Dev files go in `Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752/` |\n53\t\n54\t---\n55\t\n56\t## 2. Killer7 → Skiller7: what we keep, what is ours\n57\t\n58\t| What makes Killer7 distinctive | Skiller7 (original) |\n59\t|---|---|\n60\t| Hold one button to run along fixed rails; junction icons; a quick 180° turn | Rails through hotel halls and village lanes; **downhill ski rails** with momentum and hockey stops; **lifts** (T-bar, gondola) you can shoot from; **ski jumps**; an **avalanche chase** |\n61\t| Stop, aim in first person, scan to reveal invisible laughing suicide bombers, weak spots that kill at once | **Whiteouts**: Föhn-touched people who laugh themselves invisible. You hear them, see their **footprints in the snow and their breath**, and a **Scan** pulse reveals them. Shoot the glowing **Warm Spot** and they thaw on the spot (\"THAW.\") |\n62\t| One body, seven personas, each with a gun and a field ability | **Oma Holle's Seven**: a ski patrolman, a downhill racer, a telemark aristocrat, a fur-coated tycoon, a yodeler, a press photographer and an avalanche engineer |\n63\t| A cleaner who retrieves fallen personas | **The Sweep** skis in from Room 7 and chips frozen personas out of the ice |\n64\t| A save room with a maid and a TV | **Room 7**: Sister Rosalind, the **Cuckoo Clock** (save), the **Wax Bench** (upgrades: you wax your skills), the **Wardrobe** of seven coats (persona select) |\n65\t| Thin blood heals, thick blood buys upgrades | **Red** (shared; heals and fuels skills) and **Rime** (upgrade crystals) |\n66\t| A cryptic ghost and a grotesque hint-giver | **Pim the Lift Boy** (the ghost of a 1926 lift attendant on a floating chairlift seat) and **Murmel** (a resort-mascot marmot bungeed to a ski rack: \"Gratuity is customary.\") |\n67\t| A handler on the phone, political allegory, an eternal game between two gods | **The Concierge** (hotel euphemisms for murder), the **Thaw Accord**, Oma Holle against **Mister Föhn** over a chessboard of ice |\n68\t| Cel shading, flat colour, heavy black shadows, huge type | Comic-cel filter and ink. White-on-white snow made readable by ink outlines. Red on snow. Huge title cards |\n69\t| Chapters called targets, each ending in a strange boss | **Target 00** (the Bellcaptain in an elevator shaft). **Target 01** (an oompah band you shoot on the beat; the Powder King: a laughing snowman colossus, a slalom duel, and an interview on a stuck chairlift) |\n70\t\n71\t---\n72\t\n73\t## 3. Deliverables, folders, ground rules\n74\t\n75\t**At the end, this folder holds:** TASK.md, STAGE.md (if present), PLAN.md, the built game\n76\t`Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752.html`, `exit-interview.json` beside it, and the dev folder\n77\t`Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752/` with every source, tool and test. If your TASK.md gives the\n78\tnames with a different model or date, use your TASK.md's names (GameTitle `Skiller7`, SOURCE `Killer7`).\n79\t\n80\t**Shared machine rules (from TASK.md):**\n81\t- Read only the files you need. Other agents share this directory, the machine's ports and its processes.\n82\t- Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. Only the AI Hub harness needs a server. Start it with\n83\t  `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` (it prints the port), check that the served page really is your game\n84\t  (its `<title>` starts with \"Skiller7\"), and stop it by its own PID. Never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever\n85\t  holds a port.\n86\t- `/tmp` on this machine is a small tmpfs and may be full. Keep large files (vendor copies, screenshots, logs) inside\n87\t  the dev folder.\n88\t- Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n89\t- One test command prints only failures and a one-line count (§14). Rerun it only after changing code it covers. Use\n90\t  screenshots only for what a script can't check.\n91\t\n92\t---\n93\t\n94\t## 4. Tech stack and pinned sources\n95\t\n96\t**Pinned commits** (the repositories move daily; pin them for reproducibility):\n97\t- my-3d2dge: `4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72` (engine v0.85.2). Raw files come from\n98\t  `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72/<path>`\n99\t- ai-hub: `f8835e4824fbf9bbb4bac3a35e88e3ab197fdd1e` (session client 1.4.0). Raw files come from\n100\t  `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/f8835e4824fbf9bbb4bac3a35e88e3ab197fdd1e/<path>`\n101\t\n102\t`tools/fetch-deps.mjs` downloads these once, idempotently:\n103\t\n104\t| What | Repo path | Dev-folder path | Shipped? |\n105\t|---|---|---|---|\n106\t| **The whole handoff folder**, layout kept so its tools, kits and tests run unchanged: the 2D engine (full, readable, every module: story, rpg, songs, arcade…), `modules/`, `kits/`, `game/`, `tools/`, `docs/`, `src/assets/` (the store), `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md`, `package.json` | `dist/handoff/` (a sparse checkout at the pinned commit, or the GitHub tree API plus raw downloads) | `engine/` | only `engine/my-3d2dge.js` is inlined |\n107\t| Asset store sets we use | `dist/handoff/src/assets/sets/{kenney-holiday,kenney-furniture,kaykit-furniture-bits,kenney-nature}.js` | `engine/src/assets/sets/` (part of the copy above) | inlined: only the entries we use, cut with `engine/tools/asset-set.mjs` |\n108\t| 3D module (ES module, header = its manual) | `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` | `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` | inlined as a module |\n109\t| 3D reference slices (read, don't ship) | `src/starter3d/00-shell.js`, `30-shooter.js`, `40-horror.js`, `60-flight.js` | `ref/` | no |\n110\t| three.js r182 (tests only) | `vendor/three-0.182.0/three.webgpu.min.js`, `three.core.min.js`, `three.tsl.min.js` | `vendor/three-0.182.0/` | no (CDN) |\n111\t| Rapier 0.19.3 (tests only) | `vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/rapier.js` | `vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/` | no (CDN) |\n112\t| AI Hub client | ai-hub `lib/hub/session.js` | `vendor/aihub/session.js` | inlined **unmodified**, first script in `<head>` |\n113\t| AI Hub harness (tests only) | ai-hub `lib/hub/harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` | `vendor/aihub/` | no |\n114\t\n115\t**Runtime import map (shipped).** All four URLs were checked: HTTP 200, `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`.\n116\t```json\n117\t{ \"imports\": {\n118\t  \"three\":        \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n119\t  \"three/webgpu\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n120\t  \"three/tsl\":    \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.tsl.min.js\",\n121\t  \"@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs\" } }\n122\t```\n123\t`three.webgpu.min.js` imports `./three.core.min.js` beside it on the CDN. Rapier's compat build embeds its wasm, so\n124\tnothing else is fetched. Inline module scripts and import maps work from `file://`, so the single file plays when\n125\tdouble-clicked (online). The AI Hub localizes these engine URLs when it ingests a game (its shelf has three 0.180–0.186.1\n126\tand Rapier 0.19.3). Tests route the URLs to the `vendor/` copies, so they never depend on the network.\n127\t\n128\t**Tooling:** Node 24 (on the machine), Python 3 (the harness server only), Playwright **1.63.0** (pinned as in the\n129\thandoff's package.json) with `npx playwright install chromium`. No other libraries, no bundler: the build script\n130\tconcatenates (§5.8).\n131\t\n132\t**Why the 3D module.** Killer7 is cinematic third-person rails plus first-person aiming. The handoff's 2D views can't do\n133\tfirst person. The engine's own 3D module (the same engine and release, documented as its 3D side) gives exactly what\n134\twe need: `'fixed'` cameras we pose ourselves, a first-person overlay for the gun, `world.project` for the HUD, fog,\n135\tlights, shadows, terrain for ski runs, the 2D engine's rigs drawn as cards, and a built-in **Comic cel** filter. The 2D\n136\tengine still runs everything else: scenes, the fixed-step clock, input and rebinding, the pause menu and settings,\n137\tsaves, the Story and Dialog modules, the music clock, the HUD font and widgets.\n138\t\n139\t---\n140\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4e34a208-b0be-46a3-80b7-32b0075578cc","uuid":"f3959bb6-ab30-421f-8f37-05c8240fa8ea","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:34:38.943Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174752__xfb4127/work/PLAN.md","content":"# SKILLER7\n\n*Seven Killers, One Mountain.*\n\n**Title.** Picked the way TASK.md says: 50 playful riffs on \"Killer7\" were brainstormed silently, then one was drawn\nwith a real random number generator (Python `secrets.randbelow(50)`, OS entropy): index 13, **Skiller7**. It reads three\nways at once (SKI + KILLER + SKILL), and the whole design leans on that. In file and folder names, GameTitle is\n`Skiller7` and SOURCE is `Killer7`.\n\n**Pitch.** Piz Nada is a neutral micro-state on one mountain, and it has snowed there for a hundred years. On the top\nfloor of its sanatorium, in Room 7, a 104-year-old former ski champion called Oma Holle lies under a mountain of\nfeatherbed. When she shakes it, seven killers step out of her wardrobe. The Canton's Department of Discretion sends\nthem \"guests\" to check out: agents of Mister Föhn, the warm wind that makes people laugh until they disappear. You move\non rails through a grand hotel, a ski village, a summit conference, a black ski run, a gondola and a glacier vault.\nThen you stop, look through your persona's eyes, scan for the laughing, invisible Whiteouts, and shoot their Warm Spot\nbefore they reach you.\n\n---\n\n## 0. How to use this plan\n\n- **TASK.md + PLAN.md are the whole brief.** TASK.md decides deliverables and process. This plan decides design. Where\n  this plan gives a number, use it as the default and expose the important ones as live dev sliders (§7.22).\n- **Everything in the game is original.** We take Killer7's *structure* (rail movement, stop-and-aim first-person\n  scanning, weak-point instant kills, one body with several killer personas, a save room, a blood economy, cryptic\n  hint-givers, chapters called targets, bosses with odd rules) and its *tone*. We do not use its names, characters,\n  art, music, levels or look.\n- **Build in this order:** a playable greybox golden path first, then systems to full depth, then content, then polish\n  (§12). The scope is sized to be built whole. TASK.md forbids TODOs, placeholders and stubs, so build every system and\n  piece of content in this plan.\n- **Mark every engine change** in the engine files with `// S7 PATCH:` and a reason, and keep a list for the\n  hand-back's Engine Feedback (§16).\n\n---\n\n## 1. At a glance\n\n| | |\n|---|---|\n| Title | **SKILLER7** (shown with that spelling; `Skiller7` in file names) |\n| Tagline | Seven Killers, One Mountain |\n| Source | Killer7 (Grasshopper Manufacture / Capcom, 2005) |\n| Genre | On-rails action-adventure shooter: rail movement, first-person scan-and-shoot, persona-switch puzzles, boss fights. AI Hub catalogue genre: **Shooter** |\n| Perspective | Third-person cinematic rail cameras (fixed, dolly, follow and crane shots that hard-cut) while moving; **first person** while aiming; a schematic top-down map in the pause menu |\n| Players | 1 in the campaign. 1–4 in **Echo Run** (AI Hub ghost mode; solo, you race your own recorded echo) |\n| Length | about 45–60 min: Target 00 \"Turn-Down Service\" (about 12 min), Target 01 \"Après\" (35–45 min), epilogue. Echo Run rounds take about 2 min |\n| Engine | my-3D2dge **v0.85.2** handoff (the 2D engine runs the game) plus the engine's own **3D module v0.85.2** (three.js r182, Rapier 0.19.3 from a CDN) |\n| Look | The engine's native style: PS1-resolution picture (`'pixels'`), characters as pixel-art rigs drawn on cards, the 3D module's **Comic cel** filter (flat bands of light, ink lines). Stark white snow, black shadows, saturated red |\n| Animation | The engine's procedural rigs (`E.Humanoid`, `E.Blob`, `E.MOVES`, poses), extended with new hats, accessories, weapons, skis and poses; first-person weapon models with the overlay pen |\n| Audio | All in code: engine chip-synth songs, engine SFX voices, our own WebAudio laughter synth and Animalese-style gibberish voices |\n| Online | AI Hub Session Protocol v1, `ghost` mode (Echo Run). On its own, the game runs without the hub |\n| Ship | `Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752.html` and `exit-interview.json` in this folder. Dev files go in `Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752/` |\n\n---\n\n## 2. Killer7 → Skiller7: what we keep, what is ours\n\n| What makes Killer7 distinctive | Skiller7 (original) |\n|---|---|\n| Hold one button to run along fixed rails; junction icons; a quick 180° turn | Rails through hotel halls and village lanes; **downhill ski rails** with momentum and hockey stops; **lifts** (T-bar, gondola) you can shoot from; **ski jumps**; an **avalanche chase** |\n| Stop, aim in first person, scan to reveal invisible laughing suicide bombers, weak spots that kill at once | **Whiteouts**: Föhn-touched people who laugh themselves invisible. You hear them, see their **footprints in the snow and their breath**, and a **Scan** pulse reveals them. Shoot the glowing **Warm Spot** and they thaw on the spot (\"THAW.\") |\n| One body, seven personas, each with a gun and a field ability | **Oma Holle's Seven**: a ski patrolman, a downhill racer, a telemark aristocrat, a fur-coated tycoon, a yodeler, a press photographer and an avalanche engineer |\n| A cleaner who retrieves fallen personas | **The Sweep** skis in from Room 7 and chips frozen personas out of the ice |\n| A save room with a maid and a TV | **Room 7**: Sister Rosalind, the **Cuckoo Clock** (save), the **Wax Bench** (upgrades: you wax your skills), the **Wardrobe** of seven coats (persona select) |\n| Thin blood heals, thick blood buys upgrades | **Red** (shared; heals and fuels skills) and **Rime** (upgrade crystals) |\n| A cryptic ghost and a grotesque hint-giver | **Pim the Lift Boy** (the ghost of a 1926 lift attendant on a floating chairlift seat) and **Murmel** (a resort-mascot marmot bungeed to a ski rack: \"Gratuity is customary.\") |\n| A handler on the phone, political allegory, an eternal game between two gods | **The Concierge** (hotel euphemisms for murder), the **Thaw Accord**, Oma Holle against **Mister Föhn** over a chessboard of ice |\n| Cel shading, flat colour, heavy black shadows, huge type | Comic-cel filter and ink. White-on-white snow made readable by ink outlines. Red on snow. Huge title cards |\n| Chapters called targets, each ending in a strange boss | **Target 00** (the Bellcaptain in an elevator shaft). **Target 01** (an oompah band you shoot on the beat; the Powder King: a laughing snowman colossus, a slalom duel, and an interview on a stuck chairlift) |\n\n---\n\n## 3. Deliverables, folders, ground rules\n\n**At the end, this folder holds:** TASK.md, STAGE.md (if present), PLAN.md, the built game\n`Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752.html`, `exit-interview.json` beside it, and the dev folder\n`Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752/` with every source, tool and test. If your TASK.md gives the\nnames with a different model or date, use your TASK.md's names (GameTitle `Skiller7`, SOURCE `Killer7`).\n\n**Shared machine rules (from TASK.md):**\n- Read only the files you need. Other agents share this directory, the machine's ports and its processes.\n- Prefer `file://` and the engine's headless tools. Only the AI Hub harness needs a server. Start it with\n  `python3 -m http.server 0 --bind 127.0.0.1` (it prints the port), check that the served page really is your game\n  (its `<title>` starts with \"Skiller7\"), and stop it by its own PID. Never pkill, killall, fuser -k, or kill whatever\n  holds a port.\n- `/tmp` on this machine is a small tmpfs and may be full. Keep large files (vendor copies, screenshots, logs) inside\n  the dev folder.\n- Don't use subagents or workflows to debate, review, judge or audit.\n- One test command prints only failures and a one-line count (§14). Rerun it only after changing code it covers. Use\n  screenshots only for what a script can't check.\n\n---\n\n## 4. Tech stack and pinned sources\n\n**Pinned commits** (the repositories move daily; pin them for reproducibility):\n- my-3d2dge: `4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72` (engine v0.85.2). Raw files come from\n  `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/my-3d2dge/4390a9852fe0ad01e1699a160f2a331931bb1a72/<path>`\n- ai-hub: `f8835e4824fbf9bbb4bac3a35e88e3ab197fdd1e` (session client 1.4.0). Raw files come from\n  `https://raw.githubusercontent.com/michaelcrosato/ai-hub/f8835e4824fbf9bbb4bac3a35e88e3ab197fdd1e/<path>`\n\n`tools/fetch-deps.mjs` downloads these once, idempotently:\n\n| What | Repo path | Dev-folder path | Shipped? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| **The whole handoff folder**, layout kept so its tools, kits and tests run unchanged: the 2D engine (full, readable, every module: story, rpg, songs, arcade…), `modules/`, `kits/`, `game/`, `tools/`, `docs/`, `src/assets/` (the store), `API.md`, `API-INDEX.md`, `AI_GUIDE.md`, `package.json` | `dist/handoff/` (a sparse checkout at the pinned commit, or the GitHub tree API plus raw downloads) | `engine/` | only `engine/my-3d2dge.js` is inlined |\n| Asset store sets we use | `dist/handoff/src/assets/sets/{kenney-holiday,kenney-furniture,kaykit-furniture-bits,kenney-nature}.js` | `engine/src/assets/sets/` (part of the copy above) | inlined: only the entries we use, cut with `engine/tools/asset-set.mjs` |\n| 3D module (ES module, header = its manual) | `dist/my-3d2dge-3d.js` | `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js` | inlined as a module |\n| 3D reference slices (read, don't ship) | `src/starter3d/00-shell.js`, `30-shooter.js`, `40-horror.js`, `60-flight.js` | `ref/` | no |\n| three.js r182 (tests only) | `vendor/three-0.182.0/three.webgpu.min.js`, `three.core.min.js`, `three.tsl.min.js` | `vendor/three-0.182.0/` | no (CDN) |\n| Rapier 0.19.3 (tests only) | `vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/rapier.js` | `vendor/rapier3d-simd-compat-0.19.3/` | no (CDN) |\n| AI Hub client | ai-hub `lib/hub/session.js` | `vendor/aihub/session.js` | inlined **unmodified**, first script in `<head>` |\n| AI Hub harness (tests only) | ai-hub `lib/hub/harness.html`, `bridge.js`, `round.js` | `vendor/aihub/` | no |\n\n**Runtime import map (shipped).** All four URLs were checked: HTTP 200, `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`.\n```json\n{ \"imports\": {\n  \"three\":        \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n  \"three/webgpu\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.webgpu.min.js\",\n  \"three/tsl\":    \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/three@0.182.0/build/three.tsl.min.js\",\n  \"@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat\": \"https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@dimforge/rapier3d-simd-compat@0.19.3/rapier.mjs\" } }\n```\n`three.webgpu.min.js` imports `./three.core.min.js` beside it on the CDN. Rapier's compat build embeds its wasm, so\nnothing else is fetched. Inline module scripts and import maps work from `file://`, so the single file plays when\ndouble-clicked (online). The AI Hub localizes these engine URLs when it ingests a game (its shelf has three 0.180–0.186.1\nand Rapier 0.19.3). Tests route the URLs to the `vendor/` copies, so they never depend on the network.\n\n**Tooling:** Node 24 (on the machine), Python 3 (the harness server only), Playwright **1.63.0** (pinned as in the\nhandoff's package.json) with `npx playwright install chromium`. No other libraries, no bundler: the build script\nconcatenates (§5.8).\n\n**Why the 3D module.** Killer7 is cinematic third-person rails plus first-person aiming. The handoff's 2D views can't do\nfirst person. The engine's own 3D module (the same engine and release, documented as its 3D side) gives exactly what\nwe need: `'fixed'` cameras we pose ourselves, a first-person overlay for the gun, `world.project` for the HUD, fog,\nlights, shadows, terrain for ski runs, the 2D engine's rigs drawn as cards, and a built-in **Comic cel** filter. The 2D\nengine still runs everything else: scenes, the fixed-step clock, input and rebinding, the pause menu and settings,\nsaves, the Story and Dialog modules, the music clock, the HUD font and widgets.\n\n---\n","numLines":140,"startLine":1,"totalLines":2112}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01Wr5Tnw7bCCJpwNG9wJJQDp","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
