{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BGADGwuUpLQdKK4Gqx7ayj","type":"tool_result","content":"340\t    { id: 'j3-g1', a: 'j3', b: 'g1', kind: 'walk', gate: 'rope', cams: [ … ] }\n341\t  ],\n342\t  gates:    { rope: { kind: 'buyout', price: 20, who: 'rurik', label: 'MEMBERS ONLY' } },\n343\t  spots:    [ { id: 'murmel', node: 's1', kind: 'npc', who: 'murmel', talk: 'murmel_00A' },\n344\t              { id: 'pin1', edge: 'j2-j4', s: .7, kind: 'pickup', item: 'pin1' },\n345\t              { id: 'phone_bar', node: 'b1', kind: 'phone', talk: 'concierge_bar' } ],\n346\t  spawns:   { e1: [12, 6], e2: [14, 6] },        // tile coordinates of spawn points\n347\t  waves:    { lobby1: { spawn: [['giggler', 'e1', 0], ['giggler', 'e2', 1.5]], until: 'clear' } },\n348\t  triggers: [ { id: 'tut_aim', edge: 'j1-j2', s: .6, once: true, beat: 't00_first_whiteout' },\n349\t              { id: 'w_elev', node: 'j4', once: true, wave: 'lobby1' } ],\n350\t  props:    [ ['shape', 'reception-desk', 31, 18, 0, { facing: Math.PI }], ['thing', 'chandelier', 20, 14, 5.6],\n351\t              ['light', 20, 14, 5.2, { color: '#ffd8a0', reach: 18, power: 1.2, flicker: .05 }] ],\n352\t  blocks:   [ [x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1] ]          // extra line-of-sight blockers (in tiles/metres): desks, cabins\n353\t});\n354\t```\n355\t- **Camera shots** (per edge, in `cams`; `rcams` when walking b→a, else the same list). Every shot has `s: [s0, s1]`, its\n356\t  share of the edge's length; shots must cover [0, 1].\n357\t  - `fixed`: `at`, `look` ('hero' | [x, y, z]), `lookUp` (metres above the feet, default 1.1), `fov`.\n358\t  - `dolly`: `path` (its own points), the camera slides along it as the hero moves through s0..s1; `look`, `fov`.\n359\t  - `follow`: `back`, `side`, `up`, `ahead` (metres), `fov`, `lag` (s). The validator's default for an uncovered\n360\t    range is `follow { back: 3.6, side: 1.1, up: 1.9, ahead: 2.5, fov: 55, lag: .12 }`.\n361\t  - `crane`: `at`, `zFrom`, `zTo` (metres over s0..s1), `look`, `fov`.\n362\t  - `pov`: forced first person (a dramatic corridor).\n363\t  - `blend`: seconds of ease between this shot and the last; the default is 0, a hard cut (the Killer7 way).\n364\t- **Beats** (`S7.beat(id, function* (G) { … })`) are `game.script` generators: dialogs (`yield G.say(node)`),\n365\t  camera overrides (`yield G.cam(shot, seconds)`), waits, spawns, flags, cards (`yield G.card('TARGET 00', …)`). The\n366\t  story's nodes and every line of text live in `content/story/*.js` as `E.Story` nodes.\n367\t- **Personas, weapons, enemies, items, tuning, songs, voices** are registries with plain objects (§7, §10, §11).\n368\t  Adding a persona, an enemy or an area never changes code in `src/`.\n369\t\n370\t### 5.6 Events (`S7.bus.on(type, fn)` / `emit`)\n371\t\n372\t`shot {persona, weapon, from, dir, hit}` · `hit {target, part: 'body'|'warm'|'shield'|'prop', dmg}` · `crit {enemy}` ·\n373\t`kill {enemy, type, crit}` · `explode {at, radius, dmg, source}` · `reveal {enemy, by}` · `scan {at, radius}` ·\n374\t`laugh {enemy, style, at, loud}` · `pickup {kind, amount, at}` · `heal {amount}` · `hurt {persona, dmg, from}` ·\n375\t`freeze {persona, at}` · `rescue {persona}` · `switch {from, to}` · `junction {node, options}` · `gate {gate, ok,\n376\treason}` · `skill {persona, skill}` · `area {from, to}` · `room7 {in}` · `save {slot}` · `beat {id}` ·\n377\t`boss {id, phase}` · `music {cue}` · `tutorial {id}` · `result {target, stats, rank}` · `echo {kind}`.\n378\tAudio, VFX, HUD splashes, rumble and tutorials only listen. They never change sim state.\n379\t\n380\t### 5.7 The test and tool handle: `game.debug`\n381\t\n382\t```\n383\tsnapshot() → { scene, mode, area, node, edge, s, dir, speed, persona, hp, red, rime, items, pins, edelweiss,\n384\t               flags (story vars), objective, enemies: { alive, revealed }, boss: { id, phase, hp }, aim: { yaw, pitch },\n385\t               target, results, time, hash }\n386\tteleport(area, node) · setPersona(id) · give(item, n) · addRed(n) · addRime(n) · tune(key, v) · cheat(name, on)\n387\tkillAll() · revealAll() · freeze(personaId) · wave(id) · beat(id) · boss(id, phase) · skipCutscene() · hash()\n388\tbot: { run(route), stop(), status() }\n389\t```\n390\t`hash()` is a stable hash of the sim state (walker, roster, enemies, economy, flags, rng positions). `game.stateHash`\n391\treturns it too, so the engine's recorded sessions (`?record=1`, `engine/tools/replay.mjs`) stop at the first frame\n392\twhere the game diverges.\n393\t\n394\t### 5.8 Build (`node tools/build.mjs`)\n395\t\n396\t1. Read `engine/my-3d2dge.js`, `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js`, `vendor/aihub/session.js` and the asset-store sets we use (cut\n397\t   down to the entries we use).\n398\t2. Concatenate the bundle in this order: `src/0*`–`src/8*`, `content/**` (sorted), `src/9*`. Wrap it in\n399\t   `(() => { 'use strict'; … })();`, with the namespace at `globalThis.S7`.\n400\t3. Fill the page template (§5.1). The template fills `<title>`, the version line, the build date and the engine\n401\t   versions. The boot splash markup comes from the engine itself: load `engine/my-3d2dge.js` in a vm and call\n402\t   `E.bootSplash('SKILLER7')`.\n403\t4. Write `../Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752.html` (the root) and `out/bundle.js` (for the Node\n404\t   tests).\n405\t5. Fail the build if the bundle contains `TODO`, `FIXME`, `TBD`, `XXX`, `lorem` or `placeholder` (case-insensitive,\n406\t   outside the engine), or if `tools/validate-content.mjs` reports an error.\n407\t\n408\t### 5.9 Planned engine changes (`// S7 PATCH:` each)\n409\t\n410\t1. **Humanoid hats** (`_hat` in `engine/my-3d2dge.js`, around line 4628, next to `'cap'`, `'helmet'`, `'band'`): add\n411\t   `'beanie'` (a disc and a pompom ball: `h.pom`), `'ushanka'` (a fur disc and ear flaps), `'felt'` (a Tyrolean hat:\n412\t   brim, crown, feather `h.feather`), `'beret'` (a flattened disc tilted to one side), `'hardhat'` (a helmet with a brim\n413\t   and a headlamp glow), `'racehelmet'` (a full helmet with a visor in `h.visor`), `'pillbox'` (a bellhop cap).\n414\t2. **Humanoid accessories** (a new `acc` option drawn in the head block after the eyes, and on the torso): `goggles`\n415\t   (a band and two lenses), `visor`, `moustache`, `beard`, `scarf` (a neck ring and two tails that trail with motion),\n416\t   `shades`, `cigar` (with an ember glow), `badge`, `bandolier`, `pack`.\n417\t3. **Humanoid weapons** (in the `o.weapon` branch, beside `'gun'`): `'revolver'`, `'poles'` (two ski poles, one in\n418\t   each hand, barrels at the tips), `'rifle'` (long, with a scope), `'shotgun'`, `'alphorn'` (a long wooden horn: on the\n419\t   back in rail mode, held forward with `point: true`), `'camera'` (a box with a flash), `'launcher'` (a tube with a gas\n420\t   bottle), `'pistol'` (small).\n421\t4. **Skis** (a new option `skis: { color }`): two long lines under the feet along the facing, tips curled, drawn only\n422\t   while `rig.state.skis` is true.\n423\t5. **Default button symbols: PlayStation.** In `STANDARD_SETTINGS.glyphs` (around line 2999), set the default `value`\n424\t   to `'playstation'`. The option list stays (Xbox, PlayStation, Nintendo, Positions).\n425\t6. **AI Hub rule:** ignore `pause` from Pad9 (Options) while Pad8 (Create) is held, only when `AIHub.active`.\n426\t\n427\tEvery rig change must draw on cards (the 3D world) and in `rig.drawPortrait` (dialogs) alike. Check each one with\n428\t`engine/tools/filmstrip.mjs` on a small test page. Then run `node engine/kits/test.mjs` (the kit pages load\n429\t`../my-3d2dge.js`, which is our patched engine) and `node engine/game/test.mjs` to prove the 2D kits and the template\n430\tstill pass. The 3D module stays unpatched unless a blocker shows up; record any change there too.\n431\t\n432\t---\n433\t\n434\t## 6. Controls and 10-foot UX\n435\t\n436\t### 6.1 The action map\n437\t\n438\tThe engine's custom input preset (codes: `KeyW`, `Mouse0` left, `Mouse1` middle, `Mouse2` right, `Pad0`…`Pad15` in\n439\tthe standard mapping). Every action can be rebound on the standard Controls screen.\n440\t\n441\t| Action (id) | DualSense (Xbox glyphs) | Keyboard and mouse | Mode |\n442\t|---|---|---|---|\n443\t| Move (`move`, hold) | ✕ (A) or left stick up | W / ↑ | rail, junction (go), ski (tuck) |\n444\t| Quick turn / hockey stop (`turn`) | ○ (B) or a flick down on the left stick | S / ↓ | rail / ski |\n445\t| Pick a branch (`left`/`right`) | left stick or d-pad ← → | A D / ← → / hover a marker (a click goes) | junction |\n446\t| Interact / check (`interact`) | △ (Y) | E or F / click the object | rail |\n447\t| Heal (`heal`, hold) | □ (X) | H | rail |\n448\t| Featherbed ring (`ring`, hold; tap = last persona) | L1 (LB) | Tab (hold; aim at a portrait with the mouse) · 1–7 pick directly | rail |\n449\t| Skill (`skill`) | R1 (RB) | Q | rail, aim |\n450\t| Aim (`aim`, hold or toggle) | L2 (LT) | right mouse | rail, ski (after stopping), lift |\n451\t| Fire (`fire`) | R2 (RT) | left mouse | aim |\n452\t| Look / aim | right stick (left stick at 60%) | mouse (look or pointer mode, §7.3) | aim |\n453\t| Scan (`scan`) | L1 (LB) | Space or middle mouse | aim (the ring is off while aiming) |\n454\t| Reload (`reload`) | □ (X) | R | aim |\n455\t| Map (`map`) | Create (View) | M | rail |\n456\t| Pause | Options (Menu) | Esc / P | any |\n457\t| Menus | d-pad or sticks, ✕ confirm, ○ back | arrows / WASD, Enter or Space, Esc or Backspace; hover focuses, click picks | menus |\n458\t\n459\t- Contextual pairs share a button: L1 is the ring in rail mode and Scan in aim mode; □ is Heal in rail mode and\n460\t  Reload in aim mode. These modes never overlap, and the Controls screen lists each action separately.\n461\t- **Aim: hold** (default) or **toggle** (setting). Leaving aim mode returns to the rail shot with no input lost.\n462\t- **Ski edges:** move = tuck, release = carve (slower), turn = hockey stop (stops on the slope; then aim works). Move\n463\t  pushes off again.\n464\t\n465\t### 6.2 Prompts and glyphs\n466\t\n467\t- Every prompt calls `game.input.label(action)`. It follows the last device used (`input.lastDevice`): ✕ ○ □ △ L1 R1\n468\t  L2 R2 on a controller (A B X Y LB RB LT RT with the Xbox setting), key names and \"Left click\", \"Right click\" on\n469\t  keyboard and mouse. With the patch in §5.9, the default is PlayStation, and Settings → Controller → Button symbols\n470\t  offers Xbox. Our Settings screen also shows a shortcut toggle, \"Controller buttons: PlayStation / Xbox\", in group\n471\t  Game; it writes the same `glyphs` setting.\n472\t- A prompt is a glyph chip (the label in a rounded box drawn with `E.ui.box`) plus a verb in capitals: `△ CHECK  Murmel`.\n473\t  Prompts change the moment the device changes.\n474\t\n475\t### 6.3 The mouse in the world and in menus\n476\t\n477\t- **Rail mode:** hovering a junction marker highlights that branch and a click takes it. Hovering an interactable's\n478\t  marker (a diamond over it) focuses it; a click interacts. Hovering a Featherbed portrait focuses it; a click picks it.\n479\t  The UI turns each into an intent (§5.2).\n480\t- **Aim mode:** see §7.3 (look mode with a captured pointer, or pointer mode like a light gun).\n481\t- **Menus:** hover moves the focus, a click activates, a drag moves a slider, the wheel scrolls lists and changes\n482\t  focused values. The engine's E.Shell screens already work this way; our canvas widget kit (`80-ui-kit.js`) copies\n483\t  the behaviour exactly.\n484\t\n485\t### 6.4 Layout rules (a TV at 10 feet)\n486\t\n487\t- `res: 'ps1'`, `expand: true`: at least 320×240 game pixels, growing on the long side (about 427×240 on 16:9). Anchor\n488\t  every HUD piece to `r.W` and `r.H`. Keep a **5% safe margin** on every edge (TV overscan).\n489\t- Minimum text: the 5×7 font at scale 1 (on a 1080p TV that is about 31 px tall). Important text (prompts, splashes,\n490\t  names) uses scale 2; title cards use `E.font.title` at scale 3–5.\n491\t- **No dense screens:** at most 7 focusable items on a screen, at most about 40 words of body text at once, one idea per\n492\t  screen. Lists longer than 7 page with L1/R1 (Q/E, or the wheel).\n493\t- Focus is always obvious: a red bar, the item scaled ×1.1, and a tick sound. Confirm and back are the same everywhere\n494\t  (✕ and ○; Enter and Esc).\n495\t- Never rely on colour alone. Warm spots are a diamond ring as well as orange. Gates show a persona's face. Damage\n496\t  directions are shapes.\n497\t\n498\t### 6.5 Touch-only devices\n499\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4e34a208-b0be-46a3-80b7-32b0075578cc","uuid":"88787ed8-cb26-4782-9019-aade9bca8324","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:35:16.892Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174752__xfb4127/work/PLAN.md","content":"    { id: 'j3-g1', a: 'j3', b: 'g1', kind: 'walk', gate: 'rope', cams: [ … ] }\n  ],\n  gates:    { rope: { kind: 'buyout', price: 20, who: 'rurik', label: 'MEMBERS ONLY' } },\n  spots:    [ { id: 'murmel', node: 's1', kind: 'npc', who: 'murmel', talk: 'murmel_00A' },\n              { id: 'pin1', edge: 'j2-j4', s: .7, kind: 'pickup', item: 'pin1' },\n              { id: 'phone_bar', node: 'b1', kind: 'phone', talk: 'concierge_bar' } ],\n  spawns:   { e1: [12, 6], e2: [14, 6] },        // tile coordinates of spawn points\n  waves:    { lobby1: { spawn: [['giggler', 'e1', 0], ['giggler', 'e2', 1.5]], until: 'clear' } },\n  triggers: [ { id: 'tut_aim', edge: 'j1-j2', s: .6, once: true, beat: 't00_first_whiteout' },\n              { id: 'w_elev', node: 'j4', once: true, wave: 'lobby1' } ],\n  props:    [ ['shape', 'reception-desk', 31, 18, 0, { facing: Math.PI }], ['thing', 'chandelier', 20, 14, 5.6],\n              ['light', 20, 14, 5.2, { color: '#ffd8a0', reach: 18, power: 1.2, flicker: .05 }] ],\n  blocks:   [ [x0, y0, z0, x1, y1, z1] ]          // extra line-of-sight blockers (in tiles/metres): desks, cabins\n});\n```\n- **Camera shots** (per edge, in `cams`; `rcams` when walking b→a, else the same list). Every shot has `s: [s0, s1]`, its\n  share of the edge's length; shots must cover [0, 1].\n  - `fixed`: `at`, `look` ('hero' | [x, y, z]), `lookUp` (metres above the feet, default 1.1), `fov`.\n  - `dolly`: `path` (its own points), the camera slides along it as the hero moves through s0..s1; `look`, `fov`.\n  - `follow`: `back`, `side`, `up`, `ahead` (metres), `fov`, `lag` (s). The validator's default for an uncovered\n    range is `follow { back: 3.6, side: 1.1, up: 1.9, ahead: 2.5, fov: 55, lag: .12 }`.\n  - `crane`: `at`, `zFrom`, `zTo` (metres over s0..s1), `look`, `fov`.\n  - `pov`: forced first person (a dramatic corridor).\n  - `blend`: seconds of ease between this shot and the last; the default is 0, a hard cut (the Killer7 way).\n- **Beats** (`S7.beat(id, function* (G) { … })`) are `game.script` generators: dialogs (`yield G.say(node)`),\n  camera overrides (`yield G.cam(shot, seconds)`), waits, spawns, flags, cards (`yield G.card('TARGET 00', …)`). The\n  story's nodes and every line of text live in `content/story/*.js` as `E.Story` nodes.\n- **Personas, weapons, enemies, items, tuning, songs, voices** are registries with plain objects (§7, §10, §11).\n  Adding a persona, an enemy or an area never changes code in `src/`.\n\n### 5.6 Events (`S7.bus.on(type, fn)` / `emit`)\n\n`shot {persona, weapon, from, dir, hit}` · `hit {target, part: 'body'|'warm'|'shield'|'prop', dmg}` · `crit {enemy}` ·\n`kill {enemy, type, crit}` · `explode {at, radius, dmg, source}` · `reveal {enemy, by}` · `scan {at, radius}` ·\n`laugh {enemy, style, at, loud}` · `pickup {kind, amount, at}` · `heal {amount}` · `hurt {persona, dmg, from}` ·\n`freeze {persona, at}` · `rescue {persona}` · `switch {from, to}` · `junction {node, options}` · `gate {gate, ok,\nreason}` · `skill {persona, skill}` · `area {from, to}` · `room7 {in}` · `save {slot}` · `beat {id}` ·\n`boss {id, phase}` · `music {cue}` · `tutorial {id}` · `result {target, stats, rank}` · `echo {kind}`.\nAudio, VFX, HUD splashes, rumble and tutorials only listen. They never change sim state.\n\n### 5.7 The test and tool handle: `game.debug`\n\n```\nsnapshot() → { scene, mode, area, node, edge, s, dir, speed, persona, hp, red, rime, items, pins, edelweiss,\n               flags (story vars), objective, enemies: { alive, revealed }, boss: { id, phase, hp }, aim: { yaw, pitch },\n               target, results, time, hash }\nteleport(area, node) · setPersona(id) · give(item, n) · addRed(n) · addRime(n) · tune(key, v) · cheat(name, on)\nkillAll() · revealAll() · freeze(personaId) · wave(id) · beat(id) · boss(id, phase) · skipCutscene() · hash()\nbot: { run(route), stop(), status() }\n```\n`hash()` is a stable hash of the sim state (walker, roster, enemies, economy, flags, rng positions). `game.stateHash`\nreturns it too, so the engine's recorded sessions (`?record=1`, `engine/tools/replay.mjs`) stop at the first frame\nwhere the game diverges.\n\n### 5.8 Build (`node tools/build.mjs`)\n\n1. Read `engine/my-3d2dge.js`, `engine/my-3d2dge-3d.js`, `vendor/aihub/session.js` and the asset-store sets we use (cut\n   down to the entries we use).\n2. Concatenate the bundle in this order: `src/0*`–`src/8*`, `content/**` (sorted), `src/9*`. Wrap it in\n   `(() => { 'use strict'; … })();`, with the namespace at `globalThis.S7`.\n3. Fill the page template (§5.1). The template fills `<title>`, the version line, the build date and the engine\n   versions. The boot splash markup comes from the engine itself: load `engine/my-3d2dge.js` in a vm and call\n   `E.bootSplash('SKILLER7')`.\n4. Write `../Skiller7-Killer7-claude-opus-5-5-max-20261103-174752.html` (the root) and `out/bundle.js` (for the Node\n   tests).\n5. Fail the build if the bundle contains `TODO`, `FIXME`, `TBD`, `XXX`, `lorem` or `placeholder` (case-insensitive,\n   outside the engine), or if `tools/validate-content.mjs` reports an error.\n\n### 5.9 Planned engine changes (`// S7 PATCH:` each)\n\n1. **Humanoid hats** (`_hat` in `engine/my-3d2dge.js`, around line 4628, next to `'cap'`, `'helmet'`, `'band'`): add\n   `'beanie'` (a disc and a pompom ball: `h.pom`), `'ushanka'` (a fur disc and ear flaps), `'felt'` (a Tyrolean hat:\n   brim, crown, feather `h.feather`), `'beret'` (a flattened disc tilted to one side), `'hardhat'` (a helmet with a brim\n   and a headlamp glow), `'racehelmet'` (a full helmet with a visor in `h.visor`), `'pillbox'` (a bellhop cap).\n2. **Humanoid accessories** (a new `acc` option drawn in the head block after the eyes, and on the torso): `goggles`\n   (a band and two lenses), `visor`, `moustache`, `beard`, `scarf` (a neck ring and two tails that trail with motion),\n   `shades`, `cigar` (with an ember glow), `badge`, `bandolier`, `pack`.\n3. **Humanoid weapons** (in the `o.weapon` branch, beside `'gun'`): `'revolver'`, `'poles'` (two ski poles, one in\n   each hand, barrels at the tips), `'rifle'` (long, with a scope), `'shotgun'`, `'alphorn'` (a long wooden horn: on the\n   back in rail mode, held forward with `point: true`), `'camera'` (a box with a flash), `'launcher'` (a tube with a gas\n   bottle), `'pistol'` (small).\n4. **Skis** (a new option `skis: { color }`): two long lines under the feet along the facing, tips curled, drawn only\n   while `rig.state.skis` is true.\n5. **Default button symbols: PlayStation.** In `STANDARD_SETTINGS.glyphs` (around line 2999), set the default `value`\n   to `'playstation'`. The option list stays (Xbox, PlayStation, Nintendo, Positions).\n6. **AI Hub rule:** ignore `pause` from Pad9 (Options) while Pad8 (Create) is held, only when `AIHub.active`.\n\nEvery rig change must draw on cards (the 3D world) and in `rig.drawPortrait` (dialogs) alike. Check each one with\n`engine/tools/filmstrip.mjs` on a small test page. Then run `node engine/kits/test.mjs` (the kit pages load\n`../my-3d2dge.js`, which is our patched engine) and `node engine/game/test.mjs` to prove the 2D kits and the template\nstill pass. The 3D module stays unpatched unless a blocker shows up; record any change there too.\n\n---\n\n## 6. Controls and 10-foot UX\n\n### 6.1 The action map\n\nThe engine's custom input preset (codes: `KeyW`, `Mouse0` left, `Mouse1` middle, `Mouse2` right, `Pad0`…`Pad15` in\nthe standard mapping). Every action can be rebound on the standard Controls screen.\n\n| Action (id) | DualSense (Xbox glyphs) | Keyboard and mouse | Mode |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Move (`move`, hold) | ✕ (A) or left stick up | W / ↑ | rail, junction (go), ski (tuck) |\n| Quick turn / hockey stop (`turn`) | ○ (B) or a flick down on the left stick | S / ↓ | rail / ski |\n| Pick a branch (`left`/`right`) | left stick or d-pad ← → | A D / ← → / hover a marker (a click goes) | junction |\n| Interact / check (`interact`) | △ (Y) | E or F / click the object | rail |\n| Heal (`heal`, hold) | □ (X) | H | rail |\n| Featherbed ring (`ring`, hold; tap = last persona) | L1 (LB) | Tab (hold; aim at a portrait with the mouse) · 1–7 pick directly | rail |\n| Skill (`skill`) | R1 (RB) | Q | rail, aim |\n| Aim (`aim`, hold or toggle) | L2 (LT) | right mouse | rail, ski (after stopping), lift |\n| Fire (`fire`) | R2 (RT) | left mouse | aim |\n| Look / aim | right stick (left stick at 60%) | mouse (look or pointer mode, §7.3) | aim |\n| Scan (`scan`) | L1 (LB) | Space or middle mouse | aim (the ring is off while aiming) |\n| Reload (`reload`) | □ (X) | R | aim |\n| Map (`map`) | Create (View) | M | rail |\n| Pause | Options (Menu) | Esc / P | any |\n| Menus | d-pad or sticks, ✕ confirm, ○ back | arrows / WASD, Enter or Space, Esc or Backspace; hover focuses, click picks | menus |\n\n- Contextual pairs share a button: L1 is the ring in rail mode and Scan in aim mode; □ is Heal in rail mode and\n  Reload in aim mode. These modes never overlap, and the Controls screen lists each action separately.\n- **Aim: hold** (default) or **toggle** (setting). Leaving aim mode returns to the rail shot with no input lost.\n- **Ski edges:** move = tuck, release = carve (slower), turn = hockey stop (stops on the slope; then aim works). Move\n  pushes off again.\n\n### 6.2 Prompts and glyphs\n\n- Every prompt calls `game.input.label(action)`. It follows the last device used (`input.lastDevice`): ✕ ○ □ △ L1 R1\n  L2 R2 on a controller (A B X Y LB RB LT RT with the Xbox setting), key names and \"Left click\", \"Right click\" on\n  keyboard and mouse. With the patch in §5.9, the default is PlayStation, and Settings → Controller → Button symbols\n  offers Xbox. Our Settings screen also shows a shortcut toggle, \"Controller buttons: PlayStation / Xbox\", in group\n  Game; it writes the same `glyphs` setting.\n- A prompt is a glyph chip (the label in a rounded box drawn with `E.ui.box`) plus a verb in capitals: `△ CHECK  Murmel`.\n  Prompts change the moment the device changes.\n\n### 6.3 The mouse in the world and in menus\n\n- **Rail mode:** hovering a junction marker highlights that branch and a click takes it. Hovering an interactable's\n  marker (a diamond over it) focuses it; a click interacts. Hovering a Featherbed portrait focuses it; a click picks it.\n  The UI turns each into an intent (§5.2).\n- **Aim mode:** see §7.3 (look mode with a captured pointer, or pointer mode like a light gun).\n- **Menus:** hover moves the focus, a click activates, a drag moves a slider, the wheel scrolls lists and changes\n  focused values. The engine's E.Shell screens already work this way; our canvas widget kit (`80-ui-kit.js`) copies\n  the behaviour exactly.\n\n### 6.4 Layout rules (a TV at 10 feet)\n\n- `res: 'ps1'`, `expand: true`: at least 320×240 game pixels, growing on the long side (about 427×240 on 16:9). Anchor\n  every HUD piece to `r.W` and `r.H`. Keep a **5% safe margin** on every edge (TV overscan).\n- Minimum text: the 5×7 font at scale 1 (on a 1080p TV that is about 31 px tall). Important text (prompts, splashes,\n  names) uses scale 2; title cards use `E.font.title` at scale 3–5.\n- **No dense screens:** at most 7 focusable items on a screen, at most about 40 words of body text at once, one idea per\n  screen. Lists longer than 7 page with L1/R1 (Q/E, or the wheel).\n- Focus is always obvious: a red bar, the item scaled ×1.1, and a tick sound. Confirm and back are the same everywhere\n  (✕ and ○; Enter and Esc).\n- Never rely on colour alone. Warm spots are a diamond ring as well as orange. Gates show a persona's face. Damage\n  directions are shapes.\n\n### 6.5 Touch-only devices\n","numLines":160,"startLine":340,"totalLines":2115}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01BGADGwuUpLQdKK4Gqx7ayj","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
