{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_016ZNAhSzjj43hYUVeLzcA24","type":"tool_result","content":"1780\t**Dossier pages:**\n1781\t- (target file) TARGET 00 — Augustin Grau, bell captain, 41 years of service, never late, never smiled; smiling\n1782\t  since Tuesday.\n1783\t- (target file) TARGET 01 — Hansi Pulver, three Olympic golds, the face of Nada chocolate, instructor to presidents;\n1784\t  listed \"WARM FRONT — COMMANDER\".\n1785\t- Grau's employee file (00A).\n1786\t- Room service menu: the Bellcaptain's specials (00B).\n1787\t- The Thaw Accord, Article Nine (01B).\n1788\t- The Avalanche of 1926: a clipping with a photo of eight (01C).\n1789\t- Department of Discretion: Standing Orders (\"1. Discretion. 2. See 1.\") (01E).\n1790\t- The ski school price list (\"Private lesson: 80. Private lesson, no questions: 800.\") (01A ski school).\n1791\t\n1792\t### 11.11 Echo Run course (`content/echorun.js`)\n1793\t\n1794\tThe 01C downhill from st to vb. Encounters sit at fixed metres down the course: 120 (2 × Giggler), 260 (Tobogganer),\n1795\t420 (Plow on BLACK / 3 × Giggler on BLUE), 600 (2 × Tobogganer), 760 (Twins), 900 (Snowballer pair), 1,100 (3 ×\n1796\tGiggler), 1,300 (Plow), 1,450 (2 × Tobogganer). Types and warm spots come from `AIHub.rng('course')` (in a session) or\n1797\t`E.rng(seed)` (solo). The jump shortcut cuts about 9 s. A course-time HUD shows elapsed time and penalties; echoes show\n1798\twith their names.\n1799\t\n1800\t### 11.12 Attract vignettes (`content/attract.js`)\n1801\t\n1802\t1. \"HOLD TO MOVE. STOP TO KILL.\" 00A: walking, a junction, the first scan and crits.\n1803\t2. \"SEVEN KILLERS. ONE BODY.\" 01A market: the ring opens; every persona fires a shot or uses a skill on a crowd.\n1804\t3. \"SCAN. FIND THE WARM SPOT.\" 00B corridor: doors open both sides, quick turns, Twins splitting.\n1805\t4. \"LAWINE!\" 01C: the avalanche chase.\n1806\t5. \"SKY CABINS.\" 01D: the gondola fight, Cable Crawlers.\n1807\t6. \"EIN, ZWEI, DREI, VIER.\" 01B: the band boss, shot on the beat.\n1808\t7. \"THE POWDER KING.\" 01F: the Avalaugh, then the slalom.\n1809\tThen the title card for 3 s and loop.\n1810\t\n1811\t---\n1812\t\n1813\t## 12. Build order and milestones\n1814\t\n1815\tEach milestone ends with its tests green (`node tests/run.mjs`) and, where it says so, a screenshot look.\n1816\t\n1817\t**M0 — Foundations and spikes (first, before anything else).**\n1818\t- `tools/fetch-deps.mjs`: the pinned files of §4.\n1819\t- `tools/build.mjs` producing the single HTML with a hello world: a test area (a box room), a hero rig on a card, the\n1820\t  cel filter on, the import map to the CDN.\n1821\t- `tests/run.mjs` with one Node suite and one browser suite: the built file over `file://` with the CDN routed to\n1822\t  `vendor/`, `?backend=webgl`; boot completes; the canvas isn't blank; no console errors.\n1823\t- **Spike:** an actor placed by our code each step (`fly: true`, `pose: false`, `setSolid(false)`) stays exactly where\n1824\t  placed after `world.step`. Our own rig update animates it. `world.camera.fixed` set in draw is honoured.\n1825\t- **Spike:** the first-person overlay draws a pen box in the eye frame.\n1826\t- **Spike:** `world.project` matches the sim projection within 1 px.\n1827\t- **Spike:** the vm harness loads the engine and the bundle with the stand-ins (window, addEventListener, location),\n1828\t  runs `S7.start({ headless: true })` and advances 2 s.\n1829\t- **Exit:** all green. Note what the spikes taught in the dev folder's `NOTES.md`.\n1830\t\n1831\t**M1 — Rails and cameras (greybox).**\n1832\t- LevelGrid, RailGraph, Walker (walk, junction, quick turn, stairs), CameraDirector (fixed, follow, dolly), the\n1833\t  intent layer with the full input map, glyph prompts, junction markers (stick, keys, mouse).\n1834\t- A greybox version of 00A (walls and floors only).\n1835\t- Dev overlays: rails and shots.\n1836\t- **Exit:** the rails and camera tests of §7.1–7.2; a person can walk the greybox lobby with a pad and with keyboard\n1837\t  and mouse.\n1838\t\n1839\t**M2 — Aim, weapons, Whiteouts, scan (the core loop).**\n1840\t- Aim mode (pad and both mouse modes), Ignaz's and Dagny's weapons, hitscan against the LevelGrid, Gigglers and\n1841\t  Bellboys, waves, the scan, warm spots, crits, Red drops and healing, contact explosions, the laugh synth (positional),\n1842\t  laugh ripples, footprints and breath.\n1843\t- The HUD basics.\n1844\t- **Exit:** §7.3–7.7 tests; a bot clears a lobby wave honestly; a screenshot of aim mode with a revealed Giggler and its\n1845\t  warm spot.\n1846\t\n1847\t**M3 — Personas, skills, gates, death.**\n1848\t- All seven personas with their weapons, skills, first-person models and rig looks (the engine patches §5.9 1–4).\n1849\t- The Featherbed ring and switching.\n1850\t- Every gate kind (§7.13), including the code lock UI.\n1851\t- Freezing, the Sweep's rescue and game over.\n1852\t- The remaining enemy types.\n1853\t- **Exit:** §7.8–7.10 and §7.13 tests; a filmstrip of each persona's switch and reload; the kits still pass after the\n1854\t  engine patches.\n1855\t\n1856\t**M4 — Meta.**\n1857\t- Room 7 (world, loop, clock, Wax Bench, Wardrobe), saves and autosave, the economy, items and the Dossier, the map,\n1858\t  Story and Dialog with portraits, the Animalese voices, beats, title cards, letterbox, tutorials.\n1859\t- The E.Shell theme and pause items.\n1860\t- Settings (§7.21) and the glyph default patch.\n1861\t- **Exit:** §7.11–7.12 and §7.14–7.18 tests; the save round trip; the cold open plays with voices.\n1862\t\n1863\t**M5 — Target 00 complete.**\n1864\t- 00A and 00B/00C fully dressed (shapes, lights, props, palette, every camera shot).\n1865\t- Grau with all three phases.\n1866\t- Results.\n1867\t- The hotel and shaft music.\n1868\t- **Exit:** the playthrough bot finishes Target 00 honestly on Piste; screenshots of each area's main shots and each\n1869\t  Grau phase.\n1870\t\n1871\t**M6 — Target 01 complete.**\n1872\t- 01A to 01F, dressed.\n1873\t- The Jolly Four, the avalanche, the gondola, the Archive's reveal, Hansi's three phases, the epilogue and credits.\n1874\t- All songs.\n1875\t- **Exit:** the playthrough bot finishes the whole demo honestly on Piste (and with god mode on Off-Piste, to catch\n1876\t  softlocks); the validator's golden-path proof passes; screenshots.\n1877\t\n1878\t**M7 — Modes and shell.**\n1879\t- The title flow, difficulty select, attract mode (all vignettes), Echo Run (solo with your echo), the AI Hub session,\n1880\t  the dev mode panel (every page), the touch-only notice, About & Credits.\n1881\t- **Exit:** the attract loop runs 3 minutes headless with no errors and exits on input in the browser; the hub harness\n1882\t  passes (§14.5); the touch-only browser test passes; the dev sliders' tests pass.\n1883\t\n1884\t**M8 — Polish and ship.**\n1885\t- Balance passes with the bot's metrics (time to kill, damage taken per area, Red and Rime totals; aim for the\n1886\t  economy in §7.11).\n1887\t- Look notes from `check.mjs`, filmstrips, the screenshot review list (§14.6), and a full play by hand with a pad and\n1888\t  then with keyboard and mouse.\n1889\t- Write the About text and `exit-interview.json` (§16).\n1890\t- A final build and the full test run.\n1891\t\n1892\t---\n1893\t\n1894\t## 13. Risks and how to de-risk them\n1895\t\n1896\t| Risk | Why it matters | De-risk | Fallback |\n1897\t|---|---|---|---|\n1898\t| The 3D module from a CDN in one `file://` page | Everything rests on it | M0 spike, before any feature; tests route the CDN to `vendor/` | Inline the vendored three and Rapier as `data:` module URLs in the import map, rewriting three.webgpu's `./three.core.min.js` import to a bare specifier mapped there too |\n1899\t| Headless tests of a 3D game | TASK.md wants scripted playthroughs | The sim/presenter split from day one; NullPresenter; the sim never reads the presenter | none needed: Playwright covers only boot, look and the hub |"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4e34a208-b0be-46a3-80b7-32b0075578cc","uuid":"26570c26-4f00-432e-a86a-ccf15a6a5c05","timestamp":"2026-11-04T01:37:01.794Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/micha/dev/cc-matrix/runs/claude-opus-5-5-max.plan__20261103-174752__xfb4127/work/PLAN.md","content":"**Dossier pages:**\n- (target file) TARGET 00 — Augustin Grau, bell captain, 41 years of service, never late, never smiled; smiling\n  since Tuesday.\n- (target file) TARGET 01 — Hansi Pulver, three Olympic golds, the face of Nada chocolate, instructor to presidents;\n  listed \"WARM FRONT — COMMANDER\".\n- Grau's employee file (00A).\n- Room service menu: the Bellcaptain's specials (00B).\n- The Thaw Accord, Article Nine (01B).\n- The Avalanche of 1926: a clipping with a photo of eight (01C).\n- Department of Discretion: Standing Orders (\"1. Discretion. 2. See 1.\") (01E).\n- The ski school price list (\"Private lesson: 80. Private lesson, no questions: 800.\") (01A ski school).\n\n### 11.11 Echo Run course (`content/echorun.js`)\n\nThe 01C downhill from st to vb. Encounters sit at fixed metres down the course: 120 (2 × Giggler), 260 (Tobogganer),\n420 (Plow on BLACK / 3 × Giggler on BLUE), 600 (2 × Tobogganer), 760 (Twins), 900 (Snowballer pair), 1,100 (3 ×\nGiggler), 1,300 (Plow), 1,450 (2 × Tobogganer). Types and warm spots come from `AIHub.rng('course')` (in a session) or\n`E.rng(seed)` (solo). The jump shortcut cuts about 9 s. A course-time HUD shows elapsed time and penalties; echoes show\nwith their names.\n\n### 11.12 Attract vignettes (`content/attract.js`)\n\n1. \"HOLD TO MOVE. STOP TO KILL.\" 00A: walking, a junction, the first scan and crits.\n2. \"SEVEN KILLERS. ONE BODY.\" 01A market: the ring opens; every persona fires a shot or uses a skill on a crowd.\n3. \"SCAN. FIND THE WARM SPOT.\" 00B corridor: doors open both sides, quick turns, Twins splitting.\n4. \"LAWINE!\" 01C: the avalanche chase.\n5. \"SKY CABINS.\" 01D: the gondola fight, Cable Crawlers.\n6. \"EIN, ZWEI, DREI, VIER.\" 01B: the band boss, shot on the beat.\n7. \"THE POWDER KING.\" 01F: the Avalaugh, then the slalom.\nThen the title card for 3 s and loop.\n\n---\n\n## 12. Build order and milestones\n\nEach milestone ends with its tests green (`node tests/run.mjs`) and, where it says so, a screenshot look.\n\n**M0 — Foundations and spikes (first, before anything else).**\n- `tools/fetch-deps.mjs`: the pinned files of §4.\n- `tools/build.mjs` producing the single HTML with a hello world: a test area (a box room), a hero rig on a card, the\n  cel filter on, the import map to the CDN.\n- `tests/run.mjs` with one Node suite and one browser suite: the built file over `file://` with the CDN routed to\n  `vendor/`, `?backend=webgl`; boot completes; the canvas isn't blank; no console errors.\n- **Spike:** an actor placed by our code each step (`fly: true`, `pose: false`, `setSolid(false)`) stays exactly where\n  placed after `world.step`. Our own rig update animates it. `world.camera.fixed` set in draw is honoured.\n- **Spike:** the first-person overlay draws a pen box in the eye frame.\n- **Spike:** `world.project` matches the sim projection within 1 px.\n- **Spike:** the vm harness loads the engine and the bundle with the stand-ins (window, addEventListener, location),\n  runs `S7.start({ headless: true })` and advances 2 s.\n- **Exit:** all green. Note what the spikes taught in the dev folder's `NOTES.md`.\n\n**M1 — Rails and cameras (greybox).**\n- LevelGrid, RailGraph, Walker (walk, junction, quick turn, stairs), CameraDirector (fixed, follow, dolly), the\n  intent layer with the full input map, glyph prompts, junction markers (stick, keys, mouse).\n- A greybox version of 00A (walls and floors only).\n- Dev overlays: rails and shots.\n- **Exit:** the rails and camera tests of §7.1–7.2; a person can walk the greybox lobby with a pad and with keyboard\n  and mouse.\n\n**M2 — Aim, weapons, Whiteouts, scan (the core loop).**\n- Aim mode (pad and both mouse modes), Ignaz's and Dagny's weapons, hitscan against the LevelGrid, Gigglers and\n  Bellboys, waves, the scan, warm spots, crits, Red drops and healing, contact explosions, the laugh synth (positional),\n  laugh ripples, footprints and breath.\n- The HUD basics.\n- **Exit:** §7.3–7.7 tests; a bot clears a lobby wave honestly; a screenshot of aim mode with a revealed Giggler and its\n  warm spot.\n\n**M3 — Personas, skills, gates, death.**\n- All seven personas with their weapons, skills, first-person models and rig looks (the engine patches §5.9 1–4).\n- The Featherbed ring and switching.\n- Every gate kind (§7.13), including the code lock UI.\n- Freezing, the Sweep's rescue and game over.\n- The remaining enemy types.\n- **Exit:** §7.8–7.10 and §7.13 tests; a filmstrip of each persona's switch and reload; the kits still pass after the\n  engine patches.\n\n**M4 — Meta.**\n- Room 7 (world, loop, clock, Wax Bench, Wardrobe), saves and autosave, the economy, items and the Dossier, the map,\n  Story and Dialog with portraits, the Animalese voices, beats, title cards, letterbox, tutorials.\n- The E.Shell theme and pause items.\n- Settings (§7.21) and the glyph default patch.\n- **Exit:** §7.11–7.12 and §7.14–7.18 tests; the save round trip; the cold open plays with voices.\n\n**M5 — Target 00 complete.**\n- 00A and 00B/00C fully dressed (shapes, lights, props, palette, every camera shot).\n- Grau with all three phases.\n- Results.\n- The hotel and shaft music.\n- **Exit:** the playthrough bot finishes Target 00 honestly on Piste; screenshots of each area's main shots and each\n  Grau phase.\n\n**M6 — Target 01 complete.**\n- 01A to 01F, dressed.\n- The Jolly Four, the avalanche, the gondola, the Archive's reveal, Hansi's three phases, the epilogue and credits.\n- All songs.\n- **Exit:** the playthrough bot finishes the whole demo honestly on Piste (and with god mode on Off-Piste, to catch\n  softlocks); the validator's golden-path proof passes; screenshots.\n\n**M7 — Modes and shell.**\n- The title flow, difficulty select, attract mode (all vignettes), Echo Run (solo with your echo), the AI Hub session,\n  the dev mode panel (every page), the touch-only notice, About & Credits.\n- **Exit:** the attract loop runs 3 minutes headless with no errors and exits on input in the browser; the hub harness\n  passes (§14.5); the touch-only browser test passes; the dev sliders' tests pass.\n\n**M8 — Polish and ship.**\n- Balance passes with the bot's metrics (time to kill, damage taken per area, Red and Rime totals; aim for the\n  economy in §7.11).\n- Look notes from `check.mjs`, filmstrips, the screenshot review list (§14.6), and a full play by hand with a pad and\n  then with keyboard and mouse.\n- Write the About text and `exit-interview.json` (§16).\n- A final build and the full test run.\n\n---\n\n## 13. Risks and how to de-risk them\n\n| Risk | Why it matters | De-risk | Fallback |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| The 3D module from a CDN in one `file://` page | Everything rests on it | M0 spike, before any feature; tests route the CDN to `vendor/` | Inline the vendored three and Rapier as `data:` module URLs in the import map, rewriting three.webgpu's `./three.core.min.js` import to a bare specifier mapped there too |\n| Headless tests of a 3D game | TASK.md wants scripted playthroughs | The sim/presenter split from day one; NullPresenter; the sim never reads the presenter | none needed: Playwright covers only boot, look and the hub |","numLines":120,"startLine":1780,"totalLines":2118}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_016ZNAhSzjj43hYUVeLzcA24","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
